 pressure; optimized release cost and stack behavior have not been measured, so no particular production stall or stack threshold is claimed. Account-count traversal fuel is a structural termination witness, not a configured daily-driving deadline.\n\nPreserve: governing inputs, complete pending tool protocol pairs, exact source/lineage references, explicit permissions, unresolved effect truth and task-role/native-provider identity. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: long productive read/write sessions through repeated compaction and segment boundaries, source/dependency changes, non-ASCII and large evidence, interrupted checkpoint publication, then reopen the same lineage and prove that valid grounding is reused and stale targets are reread. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:24:48.544Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQOzk9DkD5BFDBkZ0JSPJ2QGRGtRMMcdYqk0PIquPryJTjNOoG6iHEip1T42ynwBlrue88BN37HzpPr0YRCbrxw8="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2657,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:24:49.063901027Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"9be039d5-a3c7-59ee-b3f6-30a8ceb7bf2a","title":"[L349] C6 context construction imposes four finite collection/content bounds before planning","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L349.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/orchestration/peritus-context/src/content.rs:81-161,233-257`, `src/content_binding.rs:18-26`, `src/graph.rs:62-122`, `src/node/metadata.rs:191-254`, `src/node/visibility.rs:107-129`.\n- Behavior: `ContextLimits` requires positive finite `usize` maxima for graph nodes, bytes per node, dependencies per node, and visibility roles. There is no unbounded variant. Content, graph, dependency, and visibility constructors reject complete inputs beyond their bound; graph size is checked before dependency analysis or context selection. Empty content and empty graphs/visibility sets are also rejected. Metadata requires positive token estimates and positive logical recency. The SHA binding boundary hashes the full supplied byte vector before checking its content bound.\n- Consequence: a caller can fail context construction on historical/source size even when a much smaller relevant subset would fit the model. These limits do not represent a timer or native provider session TTL; their concrete installed values and caller fallback must be traced separately. Unlike B1 immutable budget ceilings, this pure API permits callers to reconstruct a graph under larger limits. Digest, canonical identity, acyclicity, and authority/trust compatibility checks are distinct integrity requirements, not synthetic run-duration caps.\n- Repair direction: decouple durable context inventory from the selected/rendered frontier; admit bounded ranges and paged dependency references while preserving exact identities and provenance. Make host allocation limits explicit and recoverable without discarding the task or provider continuation. Check content size/range admission before full-buffer hashing when the complete object is already known to be inadmissible. Scope: constructors and compatibility validators completely read; selection, compaction, and installed adapters remain to be connected to this finding. No runtime reproduction or tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQJXr519qRYVNdAu3IBe+9Olw06IgbDwnFoMaSvr0UQRoHtX+CnfEqTQ46qcjkGxO2Jhs2rHU1vKutvrS4B1L3Ac="}
