{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2700,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.060441378Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"ca40855e-412d-59aa-9f11-3b61d49e0d68","comment_uuid":"f1d7c351-491a-4a7a-b9b2-343e41ff550d","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L231: Shutdown diagnostics are capped at 16 failures and clean status ignores recorded failures.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/shutdown.rs; src/shutdown/work.rs; src/shutdown/tests.rs. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\nPersist complete paged shutdown diagnostics and compute Clean only from proven settled work plus the absence of unresolved failures. Diagnostic count pressure cannot produce CorruptState or hide cleanup truth. Preserve stage ordering and publish exact per-owner remaining obligations so restart can reconcile every failed teardown.\n\nPreserve: canonical digest and schema identity, shared writer/reader admissibility, malformed-input rejection before allocation, exact causal ordering, actual provider/OS capability boundaries and preserved accepted historical evidence. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: valid boundary values and values just beyond each synthetic cap, final wrapper/receipt growth, non-ASCII strings, arbitrarily split frames/pages, malformed or conflicting data, mixed-version decode and save/reopen round trips. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQB212TZesYQ4SwJlpmP6jYqChOCdC60LjQEJRqQE/Uk/efaSrEQQtPxLegHXcF5dkfH5ejoW3SgWzZshCS8uFwY="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2701,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.062652266Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"f609f8f9-8cd4-5a63-9686-aca2a053e9f2","title":"[L232] A 64 MiB migration reserve gates startup even when the schema needs no migration","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L232.\n\n- Source: `crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/startup/migration.rs:16-24,47-50`; `crates/state/peritus-migrations/src/engine.rs:169-190`; `src/plan.rs:100-125`; `src/verified.rs`; `src/config.rs`.\n- Installed startup always preflights the latest schema **before** checking whether it is already current. Required capacity is reserve + all selected scratch bytes + the full logical database size if a selected step requires backup. With no steps, this still requires the fixed **64 MiB reserve**, so a current healthy database can be prevented from reopening despite no migration SQL or backup being needed.\n- Available capacity is **min(database filesystem free bytes, backup filesystem free bytes)**. The complete combined requirement must fit both observations even when the paths are on separate filesystems with different expenses. This is a conservative synthetic admission rule, beyond the physical capacity actually needed on each filesystem. It is not a maximum database size.\n- Startup/open/adoption/preflight repeat full SQLite integrity and foreign-key checks, and planning scans the applied migration history. These operations have no elapsed deadline here but add synchronous work as state grows; duration was not measured. Actual insufficient disk and detected corruption require honest recovery handling, independently of a reserve that applies to zero work. **Full planning/engine/startup files and planning tests read; no low-space fixture run.**\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQCcJrxaBb782hIWM6/eyR1Hy9zu/KkSv1oFn591QC+nXQzgQnsqwsapV7VMtkkSroQ1q4Nkxl6WPadoeGN+XIAI="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2702,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.065048894Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"f609f8f9-8cd4-5a63-9686-aca2a053e9f2","comment_uuid":"04d3dc8c-a1d6-4792-b7ee-033fd4c056a5","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L232: A 64 MiB migration reserve gates startup even when the schema needs no migration.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/startup/migration.rs:16-24,47-50; crates/state/peritus-migrations/src/engine.rs:169-190; src/plan.rs:100-125; src/verified.rs; src/config.rs. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\nFirst determine whether any migration work is required; a current schema needs no synthetic scratch reserve. Account actual backup, database and scratch expenses separately on their owning filesystems, retaining typed physical shortage and a recoverable wait. Keep registry/schema integrity checks and perform expensive verification through observable owned work.\n\nPreserve: exact candidate revision and artifact binding, complete finding/provenance conservation, independent reviewer evidence, truthful gate outcomes, protected user constraints and acceptance fail-closed on invalid evidence. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: large and multi-file candidates, repeated valid repair cycles, changed candidate facts, delayed reviews, local/offline verification, restart mid-gate and cancellation, proving correct repair handoff without accepting a failed candidate or rereading unchanged evidence forever. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQFxERcxgR20KXGD7/53RuzBJ29IcmJZIC5qKHfGIhB9u1hJ5sl4RjhmlFkl3dhE1VwQu45CI6+FMbwe8TLZbNA8="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2703,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.067217698Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"1a304872-a253-544a-a963-ee506e8a9d30","title":"[L233] Migration ownership has immediate contention rejection, and SQL validation uses broad lexical restrictions","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L233.\n\n- Source: `crates/state/peritus-migrations/src/engine.rs:115-152`; `src/config.rs`; `src/descriptor.rs`; `src/verified.rs`; `src/registry/validation.rs`; `src/error.rs`.\n- Opening an engine takes an immediate exclusive **try_lock** on the backup-directory migration-owner.lock; a concurrent owner is rejected with Io/Retry and there is no wait in this path. Different databases using the same backup directory share that lock. SQLite's default busy wait is **5 seconds**, separately configurable including zero; it is a storage lock wait, not an inference/session lifetime.\n- Migration source is capped at **4 MiB**, release labels at **128 printable ASCII bytes**, and versions at positive representable SQLite integers. SQL is split into uppercase lexical tokens and rejected for BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK/ATTACH/DETACH/VACUUM anywhere in the text, including comments or string literals. This can reject safe future migration source unrelated to actual transaction control. The two currently compiled SQL sources do not establish that such a false positive occurs in installed use.\n- Applied history must match every contiguous compiled version, source digest and release; newer schemas and reverse migrations are rejected. Those compatibility and transaction ownership invariants must not be confused with arbitrary source bounds or lexical false positives. The README still describes a one-version registry whereas source/tests contain two versions. **All migration implementation, metadata and SQL sources fully read; no new validation/lock test run.**\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQIiEoemJkfSuTyBBuhv2/N3N4ID2lrEkSdTZURtTD4wU7dXQ6B1BTHlDojfJ16M2t/r0CHGZmbqg+boXefQTCAA="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2704,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.069458282Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"1a304872-a253-544a-a963-ee506e8a9d30","comment_uuid":"d0f9d845-24f1-4072-9b44-beb75c125f0b","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L233: Migration ownership has immediate contention rejection, and SQL validation uses broad lexical restrictions.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: crates/state/peritus-migrations/src/engine.rs:115-152; src/config.rs; src/descriptor.rs; src/verified.rs; src/registry/validation.rs; src/error.rs. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\nUse per-database exact migration ownership with cancellable acquisition/handoff and typed SQLite contention, without arbitrary patience cutoffs. Parse SQL according to real statements and transaction ownership rather than matching tokens inside comments or literals. Preserve compiled digest/version continuity, exclusive transactions and ATTACH/transaction-control security restrictions where actually present.\n\nPreserve: committed command/event identity, exact causal and digest roots, exclusive ownership, atomic publication, reference-safe artifact retention and verified recovery from the last durable frontier. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: history beyond the reported count/byte boundaries, lock contention lasting beyond the old patience window, lost commit acknowledgements followed by later progress, torn uncommitted tails and true committed corruption, then reopen and resolve exact historical receipts. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQCNMHpLJeviTWex+AyjXz02BdEt5qOxFA+q+F0lfVDH4KxykjJIeGESdDCIAvxKKlnsDWCqbQX2vlaBSuNQlpwo="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2705,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.071629313Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"ca474a45-8ae6-5e77-bc26-70a3d2a619cf","title":"[L234] A rolled-back migration can require operator restore, after which daemon retry reuses a forbidden identity","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L234.\n\n- Source: `crates/state/peritus-migrations/src/engine/apply.rs:24-47`; `src/engine.rs:235-249,273-298,319-330`; `src/engine/restart.rs:28-67,80-116`; `src/catalog.rs:155-193,268-299`; `crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/startup/migration.rs:27-58`.\n- Every BeforeCommit failure on a backup-required plan becomes **RestoreRequired**, including failure to acquire the exclusive SQLite transaction before any migration SQL executes. Restart also maps Applying with the original schema version to RestoreBackup when backup was required. The daemon refuses to start until explicit operator restoration, although some of these cases represent no accepted schema effect or a rolled-back transaction.\n- After successful restore, the engine records **Restored**, excludes it from pending reconciliation, and rejects apply with that operation ID with “a new identity” required. The daemon always derives its migration operation ID from the same compiled registry digest. On the next startup with the restored old schema, it plans the same migration with the same ID and hits that rejection. This is a source-proven recovery dead end for that production path; the restore test stops after checking the restored version and does not exercise daemon restart/retry.\n- The API's Applied/idempotent return is also preceded by validate_plan requiring the old source version to equal the now-current schema, so reusing the original successful plan fails before reaching Applied. Replanning to the target changes from-version/backup policy and fails exact operation-plan reuse. Daemon normal current-schema startup skips apply, so that API defect is not itself evidence of every ordinary restart failing.\n- Pending operations are loaded as a whole vector; one malformed retained pending row fails the entire recovery scan. Exact registry/backup digest and accepted-effect reconciliation are necessary, but known nonacceptance, rolled-back work and restored attempts need distinct resumable states and stable lineage with a new attempt identity. **Entire recovery/catalog/apply sources and fault tests read; no tests executed.**\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQMobq3ppYEF3WbKQOTGUtcoNMGIohLwvz7V68BAtWAVM8J62XdodYAOxELItpeM/vhZUrV1xDNIXSSGUgxFF/gw="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2706,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.073868958Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"ca474a45-8ae6-5e77-bc26-70a3d2a619cf","comment_uuid":"7b3f4f33-b5f2-492f-aa96-83b582a59e6f","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L234: A rolled-back migration can require operator restore, after which daemon retry reuses a forbidden identity.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: crates/state/peritus-migrations/src/engine/apply.rs:24-47; src/engine.rs:235-249,273-298,319-330; src/engine/restart.rs:28-67,80-116; src/catalog.rs:155-193,268-299; crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/startup/migration.rs:27-58. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\nModel migration intent lineage with distinct Prepared/DefinitelyUnapplied/Applying/Applied/RolledBack/RestoreRequired/Restored attempts. Known no-effect failures can retry safely, while uncertain accepted work requires exact backup/schema reconciliation. Derive a fresh attempt identity under the same plan lineage after restoration and resolve historical committed outcomes before current-version admission; one malformed unrelated row must not hide all recoverable operations.\n\nPreserve: exact snapshot/preimage identity, protected paths, ancestor and symlink safety, writable-fork coverage, conflict detection, one logical restore transaction and exact effect receipts. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: large-file and many-path capture/restore, divergent preimage rejection, interrupted staging/publication/restore, then reopen and continue the same conversation with an exact restored workspace. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQLfU9cF40WYCnx2wWBfVc/HQ7moAn5lnfnO3q0PGrfmNfUeZT5NN01sTd+eCrCl1o59XN4GOKJJ/5cR9ivxPnAY="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2707,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.076254835Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"390df671-485a-5373-b417-f055a1fb10ce","title":"[L235] Prompt capacity includes old terminal responses, while approval time and epoch checks need explicit reissue recovery","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L235.\n\n- Source: `crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/prompt/types.rs:9-40`; `prompt/registry.rs:73-103,133-164,306-360`; `authority/owner/prompt.rs:65-102,159-168`; `authority/owner/prompt/restoration.rs:17-57`; `crates/state/peritus-approval/src/authentication.rs:151-161,251-267`.\n- The daemon prompt broker defaults to **256 retained entries**, with a configurable compiled maximum of **4,096**. Awaiting, answered and cancelled entries all count. Entries are removed only by explicit terminal retirement; no automatic paging/eviction appears in this broker. Exact owner/session listing fails when the complete set exceeds its caller bound instead of providing a continuation page.\n- Retirement only removes the in-memory entry, leaving the durable target intact. Answer/register paths can restore that old terminal target and consume a slot again. A full broker prevents restoration of an absent old target before its answer or cancellation can be processed. Production retirement/reissue callers still need full inspection before calling this a proven installed accumulation leak.\n- Signed authentication checks the request, action scope, requirement, credential and decision expiry against an epoch-bound monotonic clock; expiry is exclusive. The daemon also rejects approval challenges whose registry revision or authority epoch differs from current state. No fixed validity duration is selected in these files. User-input/cancellation admission does not require signed authority time. Necessary explicit authority validity must not become automatic loss of conversation or native model context; renewal/reissue handling remains pending.\n- **Complete prompt broker, authority target/restoration, clock, authentication and tests read; tests not run.** Errors preserve capacity versus stale authority versus invalid authentication categories, but these functions do not themselves renew a challenge.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQLaCx7Nd+Hp62LfninFiA4/NHLFSqM/hCTS9z1vvGgVuDYbAD0r/ZzPSxNiVTLB15CR/AZZ+kkxc+/XHFbeR7gc="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2708,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.078431768Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"390df671-485a-5373-b417-f055a1fb10ce","comment_uuid":"23547e96-7564-4f9b-b996-84704208bddc","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L235: Prompt capacity includes old terminal responses, while approval time and epoch checks need explicit reissue recovery.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/prompt/types.rs:9-40; prompt/registry.rs:73-103,133-164,306-360; authority/owner/prompt.rs:65-102,159-168; authority/owner/prompt/restoration.rs:17-57; crates/state/peritus-approval/src/authentication.rs:151-161,251-267. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\nSeparate active awaiting prompts from durable historical target/answer receipts with paged lookup. Answer/cancel/restore of an existing identity must not require a new ordinary capacity slot. Explicitly reissue stale/expired approval challenges against the current epoch and credential registry while preserving the original requested action and decision history; do not remove legitimate authority validity checks.\n\nPreserve: explicit user authority, credential secrecy, exact target/resource binding, fencing, scope confinement, replay rejection for changed command content and fail-closed acceptance evidence. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: renewal/adoption under the same authorized identity, revoked authority, stale epoch/revision, cross-scope requests, lost acknowledgements and restart, proving legitimate continuation without admitting unauthorized or duplicate effects. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQFcrLVSFa93uR7B3RTTLlxmqCevzp8rjjkxn714dipWpJaT4ynaa4oVvN931U8YgGHza04o0eemsEbh0cg5gBQY="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2709,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.081440683Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"4db5fe1b-52dd-594a-af41-c5ae3bbed1da","title":"[L236] Application heartbeat has a fixed three-miss cutoff and rejects late superseded replies","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L236.\n\n- Source: `crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/session/connection.rs:58-63,202-204,209-231`; `session/heartbeat.rs:11,26-59`; `session/connection/select.rs:25-40`.\n- Each connection selects a **10-second heartbeat cadence** and disconnects at **three pending missed replies**. With regular scheduling and no replies, it sends at approximately 10/20/30 seconds and fails at 40 seconds. This is an application connection liveness policy, not a model/run elapsed deadline, and the code does not offer a no-cutoff setting here.\n- Every successful heartbeat send overwrites the one pending nonce/sequence. A delayed reply to the preceding heartbeat is treated as invalid input and terminates the connection even before the three-miss ceiling. Long synchronous request handling or backpressured delivery can delay heartbeat/read service; no heartbeat-specific task runs independently from those actions.\n- Disconnect cleanup abandons artifact transfers and releases terminal attachments, while the inspected path does **not** close the durable session or cancel a product run. Whether a client reconnects with the retained session ID must be traced through that client's code before treating this as native context loss. **All four files fully read; no delayed-heartbeat fixture run.**\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQO3T6UmdaHBvbMShKWzgfVIbCSrzncLBKupYKhlgqTIdCOuWS3H6RyDCzzRChG01EwxmoYvIwraHl1c1rQAugQo="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2710,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.083603974Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"4db5fe1b-52dd-594a-af41-c5ae3bbed1da","comment_uuid":"0f4bdfa0-bf6e-403a-a173-2feb86a3284c","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L236: Application heartbeat has a fixed three-miss cutoff and rejects late superseded replies.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/session/connection.rs:58-63,202-204,209-231; session/heartbeat.rs:11,26-59; session/connection/select.rs:25-40. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\nMake heartbeat an independently serviced attachment-liveness protocol with an acknowledged nonce frontier. Keep a pending challenge until answered instead of overwriting it and rejecting delayed valid replies. Remove the mandatory three-miss disconnect policy or make an explicitly selected transport policy separate from durable task/session ownership; a reconnect reattaches the same session and recovery cursor.\n\nPreserve: durable logical session and run identity, exact command receipts, atomic snapshot/cursor binding, explicit authority, selected UI state, complete history retrieval and owned-effect reconciliation. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: large histories and non-ASCII fields, queue/connection pressure, delayed acknowledgements, subscription gaps, disconnect/reconnect and daemon restart, proving snapshot recovery, preserved selection/context, responsive controls and no duplicate command. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQFfymxKasp1Ae2k4UyFzR6mZJmfj3HPRvwkHi3/MUANM7pwSgrSg58D/sbb814BRpcyAw0lTrTUlwbOjquPN7A8="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2711,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.085857971Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"8691cf26-9cf5-5b18-af45-3e0a7b8542da","title":"[L237] Connection capacity can be occupied by uncancellable hello reads or stalled serial writes","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L237.\n\n- Source: `crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/ipc/server.rs:41-65,79-106,122-127`; `session/connection.rs:40-46,119-131,163-204`; `ipc/frame.rs:64-104,141-153`; configured connection ceilings L202.\n- The server retains a permit for each connection until run_connection returns, and pauses acceptance when all permits are held. Before entering its stop-aware action selector, each connection awaits its initial ClientHello read, authority establishment and ServerHello write without selecting the stop channel. An authenticated peer that keeps a partial/empty hello open can therefore retain a slot and prevent the server's final join from completing after stop.\n- After negotiation, request handlers and event pumps are awaited serially. Complete frame write_all/flush also awaits without selecting stop or inbound control. A client that stops reading can hold this task in a write, preventing it from processing queued heartbeat replies, cancellation, or shutdown. Removing elapsed timers alone does not repair ownership or make cancellation observable during these waits.\n- Frame reads **do** retain partial header/payload progress when cancelled; header-declared lengths are checked before allocation under the negotiated codec limit. Those integrity/allocation checks are separate from the lifecycle issue. The listener's **100 ms** retry delay prevents resource-error log spinning and is not a session timeout. **Server, connection, framing and cancellation tests fully read; no stalled-peer test run.**\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQPfRUMe3nCct/3WBxM1nCEvhhjR/QhVpwhSDdwetwmRM5E/X71CBXZ62F607bAOWqPfLx07lRJLvzTa6GdnNHwk="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2712,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.087929785Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"8691cf26-9cf5-5b18-af45-3e0a7b8542da","comment_uuid":"8884da1f-9a6b-463a-a4f7-fee2c61aa553","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L237: Connection capacity can be occupied by uncancellable hello reads or stalled serial writes.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/ipc/server.rs:41-65,79-106,122-127; session/connection.rs:40-46,119-131,163-204; ipc/frame.rs:64-104,141-153. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\nOwn negotiation, reads and writes as cancellable connection phases with independent inbound/control service and fair outgoing backpressure. A stalled hello/writer must release or detach its transport permit on explicit stop while retaining accepted logical operations for receipt reconciliation. Preserve partial-read framing progress and declared-length allocation checks without substituting a hidden timeout.\n\nPreserve: canonical digest and schema identity, shared writer/reader admissibility, malformed-input rejection before allocation, exact causal ordering, actual provider/OS capability boundaries and preserved accepted historical evidence. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: valid boundary values and values just beyond each synthetic cap, final wrapper/receipt growth, non-ASCII strings, arbitrarily split frames/pages, malformed or conflicting data, mixed-version decode and save/reopen round trips. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQMAIyGL9PISizsFF34wcrJ1VHEgU1YiNkrbZ1sQh2v8Q7tJJytUf8z9eGI0yp/iTstfS/QuqM8q70wEyEgO2fAE="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2713,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.090243005Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"923a1d06-1487-57dc-865b-41b702df255b","title":"[L238] Shutdown acknowledgement precedes queue admission, and reporting delivery can block","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L238.\n\n- Source: `crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/session/request.rs:318-349`; `session.rs:14-35`; `session/connection.rs:132-148,234-254`.\n- Shutdown response construction and socket write occur **before** try_send admits the shutdown command. If the shared shutdown queue is full or unavailable, the peer can receive ShutdownAccepted and then lose the connection without its command being admitted. Queue capacity and durable/idempotent admission need to be distinguished from accepted work; acknowledgement must reflect the actual admission boundary.\n- Each shutdown command owns an **8-event reporting channel**. Delivery awaits send and ignores its returned error. Relay awaits each event and each socket write; it exits only after ShutdownComplete or reports closure-before-completion. A nonreading peer can stop relay consumption and leave reporting delivery waiting when the channel fills. Actual teardown event count and delivery call placement still need caller verification before claiming a production capacity overflow.\n- **Request dispatch, reporting channel and relay fully read.** No new shutdown/backpressure test run, and no daemon process was changed during this audit.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQDDAdDJG8euejXb9OsHW1+OaiNEtDF9VlwhR9LCGMVTMd5+b9tHdZhJAmQYxmmUhOwcRVwJKp3gbXgBt9SLG0wU="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2714,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.092322459Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"923a1d06-1487-57dc-865b-41b702df255b","comment_uuid":"472c15e8-8ea6-4dd0-a0be-36f801c79497","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L238: Shutdown acknowledgement precedes queue admission, and reporting delivery can block.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/session/request.rs:318-349; session.rs:14-35; session/connection.rs:132-148,234-254. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\nAdmit shutdown intent durably with exact idempotency before returning ShutdownAccepted. Send progress through a durable paged reporting stream independent of one client socket or small channel, and reconcile a lost acknowledgement by historical receipt. A nonreading client can detach without blocking daemon coordination or discarding the accepted shutdown intent.\n\nPreserve: canonical digest and schema identity, shared writer/reader admissibility, malformed-input rejection before allocation, exact causal ordering, actual provider/OS capability boundaries and preserved accepted historical evidence. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: valid boundary values and values just beyond each synthetic cap, final wrapper/receipt growth, non-ASCII strings, arbitrarily split frames/pages, malformed or conflicting data, mixed-version decode and save/reopen round trips. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQEm+anLYRuhhzd2MqIpnsBGRble6PBDSchm216YMTod42lhe1uMzPedOOT8v6x4IUxbow0OVGdqaBOUkVdyhxAI="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2715,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.094703894Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"efff1a0f-39fb-5e9b-a640-9d04eb0a3a8f","title":"[L239] Discovery and subscriptions use fixed concurrency windows, and gap recovery is only admitted before progress","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L239.\n\n- Source: `crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/session/request/discovery.rs:13-16,48-70`; `subscription/pump.rs:24,39-78,127-224`; `crates/app/peritus-app-protocol/src/subscription/transitions.rs:19-45,75-99,180-241`; topic grammar in daemon `subscription/registry.rs`.\n- Model discovery is limited to **4 connection-owned tasks**, counting completed tasks until they are reaped. A fifth query is Backpressure. There is no discovery elapsed deadline here; disconnect aborts and reaps these tasks. This preserves responsiveness relative to serial request handling, but a permanently stalled catalog can occupy a slot until disconnect.\n- Subscription count is bound to the protocol's **max_topics (64 at production defaults)**, and each delivery window is at most **256 in-flight events** by default. A full window produces backpressure; pause and gap-recovery phases stop ordinary delivery. Filters use a closed registry of event families and 18 aggregate labels. These connection windows are not run lifetime quotas.\n- Every **2 seconds**, all unacknowledged events are redelivered with preserved event bytes/identity and incremented u32 attempt counts. There is no small retry-count cutoff; arithmetic overflow is a representation failure. Whole-window redelivery and all subscription pumps await socket writes serially (L237).\n- The daemon checks for a retention gap only while scanned_cursor equals the original requested cursor, and the pure transition rejects a declared gap after any scan/delivery progress. Thus this implementation cannot report a later gap through that transition. Actual source retention behavior and client snapshot/replacement handling still require their complete caller reads before claiming installed midstream loss. **All subscription implementation/proof files and discovery tests fully read; tests not run.**\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQBbys4B5P6YUEpwwcsIcmKBHcj9luW4KpN7OHGCA574G7slWvYn85I/DHHU8z+ESoBSm8GdEntGNkMf8Ve6y0gw="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2716,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.096961034Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"efff1a0f-39fb-5e9b-a640-9d04eb0a3a8f","comment_uuid":"ef3cce26-5f06-4402-85e9-04decf26a966","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L239: Discovery and subscriptions use fixed concurrency windows, and gap recovery is only admitted before progress.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/session/request/discovery.rs:13-16,48-70; subscription/pump.rs:24,39-78,127-224; crates/app/peritus-app-protocol/src/subscription/transitions.rs:19-45,75-99,180-241; subscription/registry.rs. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\nTreat discovery/subscription windows as cancellable in-flight capacity with fair queues and immediate terminal-slot reclamation. Permit retention-gap transitions at any scan frontier, replacing delivery with an atomic snapshot plus a new exact cursor. Preserve duplicate event identity and acknowledgements while serving controls independently of serial redelivery writes.\n\nPreserve: durable logical session and run identity, exact command receipts, atomic snapshot/cursor binding, explicit authority, selected UI state, complete history retrieval and owned-effect reconciliation. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: large histories and non-ASCII fields, queue/connection pressure, delayed acknowledgements, subscription gaps, disconnect/reconnect and daemon restart, proving snapshot recovery, preserved selection/context, responsive controls and no duplicate command. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQN+mLqjNqmG+q620sXTOlLAc1PEV5UpnjF3d49khvxlPU4dt4Af4qWAf9gaCfosR6LFBY3y0SVaTL+2bwYEpRAk="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2717,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.099044408Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"a616b86b-1375-5e89-b288-ad9b05287f1d","title":"[L240] Shared clients have no default request deadline, but idle requests have no heartbeat owner","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L240.\n\n- Source: `crates/app/peritus-app-client/src/timeout.rs`; `connection.rs:34-48,108-122,143-153`; `request.rs:28-74`; `events.rs:22-79,82-104`; `frame.rs:33-59,95-115`; CLI adapter `crates/app/peritus-cli/src/client.rs`.\n- The shared client accepts **Option<Duration>**, with **None = no timeout**. A supplied duration covers the full connect/negotiation or request/write/control exchange; this layer selects no synthetic default, and explicit zero is not rejected. Event reads deliberately have no request deadline. The CLI adapter forwards these settings unchanged; option selection still needs the CLI caller reads.\n- Heartbeats are replied to only during an active request or when an event consumer explicitly reads and responds. There is no idle background heartbeat task. Retaining an otherwise unused shared request connection therefore cannot keep it alive under the server's fixed cutoff (L236); a later reuse can also meet queued superseded nonces. The TUI uses its own independent reader/writer client, so this is not proof of that TUI taking the shared-client idle path.\n- Any interrupted/failed exchange invalidates the client, requiring replacement and reconciliation of the original operation receipt. Client frame reads use per-call local buffers, so cancelled reads cannot safely continue on that stream; daemon frame reads retain progress instead. Native model context and durable IPC session identities are independent of this stream replacement. Reconnect explicitly supports requesting the original active session and rejects a different returned session.\n- Windows named-pipe connect is a single open attempt; pipe-busy retry is absent here. Nonzero random transport IDs have a **4-attempt** all-zero retry ceiling, an exceptional identity-source bound rather than a daily work budget. **All eight shared-client source files and CLI adapter fully read; inline tests not run.**\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQA0F8c9QKOxVFXaa3mlxH1TdI6tTFCWb4h9J2mL+0t7hkqntSDLWVj/AQ5PfxlaOWCg2FB0oNpE7v8cPkAkQswU="}
