{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2841,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.373580391Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"246d1953-ddb5-5a97-bb95-e3bea963da2d","title":"[L391] Primitive capability names have a compiled 128-byte ASCII grammar boundary","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L391.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/foundation/peritus-types/src/capability.rs:109-186`, `src/capability.rs:206-225`, `tests/capability.rs:10-47`, `README.md:9-18`.\n- Behavior: every checked CapabilityName constructor enforces at most 128 bytes and lowercase ASCII dot-separated segments beginning with a letter and continuing with letters, digits, or hyphens. Underscores, slashes, spaces, Unicode, wildcards, uppercase, leading digits, and empty segments are rejected. Fields are private and the type exposes no larger-name configuration. Dots are ordinary canonical bytes rather than inherited authority.\n- Consequence: naming schemes outside that compiled grammar cannot be represented through this primitive even when their surrounding request fits. This is a schema/naming boundary, not a deadline, session expiry, or execution budget. Recursive validation and byte comparison are bounded by the accepted 128-byte representation and are not the unbounded collection recursion in L389. The approval rendering field cap in L385 is larger than the accepted capability name.\n- Repair direction: separate stable capability identity from human labels or namespaced external identifiers if broader names are needed; preserve canonical authority matching and checked arithmetic. All 24 crate files, including tests and manifest, were read; no tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGW