{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2949,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.630750318Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"9ab968d5-1db1-5647-bf68-2c13b2c8dca8","title":"[L455] F0 repeats whole-state and whole-store work synchronously during attribution, commit and recovery","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L455.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/analysis/peritus-evolution/src/attribution/engine.rs:123-142`, `src/campaign/reducer/transition.rs:61-63`, `src/campaign/state.rs:260-288`, `src/durability/replay.rs:79-116,127-161`, `src/wire/campaign.rs:170-199,279-281,318-321`, `src/wire/pointer.rs:178-213,299-301,338-341`, `src/runtime/publication.rs:53-83` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: Each task prediction performs an existence scan and another task/k scan through the complete pass-at-k vector. Transitions clone all nested prior campaign evidence and rebuild the collection digest. Commit prevalidates state encoding, clones it into a frame and encodes it again. Recovery loads all events, applies each reducer during frame checking and again in the replay loop, then decodes and compares the complete checkpoint. Every publication obtains a global journal integrity export and scans its artifact references before admitting evidence. These synchronous functions offer no cancellation/progress cursor.\n- Consequence: Task attribution can cost O(predictions times task/k observations), and campaign replay repeatedly copies growing embedded evidence. Pointer history bounds cap its hot state but do not make the event journal finite. These are source-derived latency and memory-pressure risks; no benchmark, active-session stall or wall timeout is attributed to F0 here.\n- Repair direction: Index task/k observations, reference immutable evidence, perform each checked reduction once and verify paged snapshots with authenticated replay cursors. Preserve integrity while providing resumable bounded work and observable progress. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQD8gCNv0ySU+kiHF6LZ96A9LC9UFLiWZvf/xhyyLtny23iynefbcX33/XlU+U4HwAlwNCBBVXMd57rbUFiXfsA8="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2950,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.632967455Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"9ab968d5-1db1-5647-bf68-2c13b2c8dca8","comment_uuid":"fa73b83c-6cd6-46bd-ae9c-96dc9fe960c2","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L455: F0 repeats whole-state and whole-store work synchronously during attribution, commit and recovery.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: crates/analysis/peritus-evolution/src/attribution/engine.rs:123-142; src/campaign/reducer/transition.rs:61-63; src/campaign/state.rs:260-288; src/durability/replay.rs:79-116,127-161; src/wire/campaign.rs:170-199,279-281,318-321; src/wire/pointer.rs:178-213,299-301,338-341; src/runtime/publication.rs:53-83. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\nIndex task/k observations, reference immutable evidence, perform each checked reduction once and verify paged snapshots with authenticated replay cursors. Preserve integrity while providing resumable bounded work and observable progress. No tests run.\n\nPreserve: exact candidate revision and artifact binding, complete finding/provenance conservation, independent reviewer evidence, truthful gate outcomes, protected user constraints and acceptance fail-closed on invalid evidence. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: large and multi-file candidates, repeated valid repair cycles, changed candidate facts, delayed reviews, local/offline verification, restart mid-gate and cancellation, proving correct repair handoff without accepting a failed candidate or rereading unchanged evidence forever. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQLK8vLSeyTrJnkGLLAQZ4znBEjQE6mMJicuUTWEWlYUAUsT71DXaoy9UThS+8JNtgKTSNxPtctTzwzcUIbfEzQw="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2951,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.635151608Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"0d117215-ab2b-55e8-99a8-29f67cda9e5e","title":"[L457] C5 request controls and identifier/media fields have additional fixed acceptance ceilings","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L457.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/model/peritus-model-protocol/src/request/options.rs:123-143`, `src/request/validation.rs:35-67`, `src/identity/public.rs`, `src/identity/sensitive.rs`, `src/content.rs:80-98,168-183`, `src/redaction.rs:28-40` in that crate; aggregate protocol maxima in L017.\n- Behavior: Generation requires positive max_output_tokens and at most 64 stop strings. Each request allows at most 128 option extensions. Provider/tool/extension/output names and idempotency keys are capped at 128 bytes, model and request/response/item/tool-call/event identities at 512, cache identities at 1,024, MIME values at 128 and media references at 8,192. These constants have no widening/unlimited option. Negotiation separately takes the minimum of five positive profile/caller numeric ceilings; request output must fit the negotiated ceiling even for a profile declaring output enforcement Advisory.\n- Consequence: Representable provider identifiers or request controls beyond these local constants are rejected before transport. The caps are schema/input acceptance limits, not session duration or cache TTL. Model capacity metadata and API-required generation controls must be distinguished from artificial execution cutoffs; no installed request hitting these exact bounds was observed here.\n- Repair direction: Ground field constraints in versioned provider contracts, keep diagnostics bounded separately, and support evolving or paged representations where the provider actually accepts more. Keep advisory output requests distinct from guaranteed enforcement. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQGytupCMOzzH5fCUaEWY7A3xESI7QO66NTfdcNly9J4iLgJY/eAW7HGiRQ7EPnh7zYA9ACfejmdgawFkIO3xQgo="}
