{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":2957,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.648876986Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"094ede4f-373c-5c19-b5dc-1dcbd983fa7c","title":"[L460] C5 canonical request identity has a separate mandatory 512-MiB total ceiling","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L460.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/model/peritus-model-protocol/src/canonical.rs:19-41,58-100`, `src/request/model.rs:165-187,197-205` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: canonical_bytes always uses a 512-MiB ceiling. canonical_bytes_bounded permits only positive smaller values. Its writer also independently caps collections at 1,100,000, strings at 32 MiB, bytes fields at 256 MiB and depth at 128. Fingerprinting and semantic idempotency encode the complete ordered conversation/tool/options request again rather than using a persisted streaming digest.\n- Consequence: Per-field-valid retained conversation can eventually become unrepresentable as one canonical request; increasing caller storage above 512 MiB is rejected. Smaller chosen ceilings are enforced incrementally before appending each field, so this path does not simply build all bytes then truncate them. Full repeated encoding remains synchronous work proportional to request size. No live request of this size was reproduced.\n- Repair direction: Keep one exact canonical identity over authenticated paged content with streaming encoding/digesting and explicit provider-context selection. Separate total retained conversation from the bounded context of an individual provider invocation. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5Mt