{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3029,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.827218906Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"1bf2f3e6-c6c1-5477-9778-59d75c06364b","title":"[L499] OpenAI stream indexing and whole-fragment assembly retain hidden bounds","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L499.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/model/peritus-provider-openai/src/stream/state.rs:71-85,103-126`; `src/stream/decode.rs:11-64,125-143`; `src/stream/output.rs:11-66,117-179,197-249,274-356`; `src/stream/terminal.rs:170-201` in the same crate; previously read provider-core tool/structured-output healing bounds.\n- Behavior: sequence zero and reordering are rejected, identical seen-sequence digests are retained for duplicate suppression, and positive gaps are accepted without detecting omitted events. Unknown correctness-critical events/items fail closed. Content indices are flattened as output_index times 65536 plus content_index without restricting the latter below 65536; overflow rejects large indices and different coordinate pairs can collide. Nonzero content parts append an identifier suffix under the same ItemId width bound. Tool arguments remain buffered until finalized, structured text is withheld until whole-value completion, and finalized text/arguments must exactly match retained deltas. Item closure scans all parts; seen sequence/item/part history remains for the whole stream. Usage input plus output must equal total when all three are present.\n- Consequence: legal-width source identities can overflow after local suffixing; flattening can collide; missing sequence events may be detected only through downstream state failure. General output bounds do not remove the narrower whole-value/fragment/argument limits, synchronous copies/parsing, or growing history scans already recorded for healing. No provider claim that zero/gaps are valid is inferred here.\n- Repair direction: define explicit provider sequence semantics, preserve collision-free tuple coordinates and bounded identifier derivation, publish meaningful buffered progress, and make assembly limits compatible with the normalized protocol and durable resume state. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQNLe6brt0Q1+Znz448wnNwnT5yU2ZXoaib49CvZ+g6hMr+cYyJCXY+Y3RA00gMXJzrugUO9xQKGiEHhXwJaFwwk="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3030,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.829605526Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"1bf2f3e6-c6c1-5477-9778-59d75c06364b","comment_uuid":"fd0d5569-c561-4786-8188-bdfd74fe7414","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L499: OpenAI stream indexing and whole-fragment assembly retain hidden bounds.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: crates/model/peritus-provider-openai/src/stream/state.rs:71-85,103-126; src/stream/decode.rs:11-64,125-143; src/stream/output.rs:11-66,117-179,197-249,274-356; src/stream/terminal.rs:170-201. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\ndefine explicit provider sequence semantics, preserve collision-free tuple coordinates and bounded identifier derivation, publish meaningful buffered progress, and make assembly limits compatible with the normalized protocol and durable resume state. No tests run.\n\nPreserve: definitely-unaccepted versus ambiguously-accepted request state, exact native thread/profile identity, ordered provider observations, tool-result protocol, explicit cancellation and real provider/model capabilities. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: long provider outages and Retry-After, split transport chunks and large streams, ambiguous acceptance and duplicate events, cancellation during recovery, adapter restart and route changes, proving same-thread reconnection and no duplicated accepted request/effect. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQIToD8jrDbRXNRs2k2SyI0JKrHkF1nh/vJZMJFYLnf7Moo+I67p7gaUZkcKOjmn5ll47R7mAL5MbyGR1BPnBogU="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3031,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.832055633Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"9d981a10-11ec-559b-9ca1-436d75290836","title":"[L502] MCP finite admission and response backpressure can block cancellation notifications","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L502.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/extensions/peritus-mcp/src/server.rs:31-62,130-143,189-263,268-281,549-560`; `src/bridge.rs:225-269` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: production limits are 2 MiB per message, 32 in-flight requests, and 128 entries per page; limits must be positive. Saturated admission immediately returns an internal error instead of queuing work. Responses share a bounded queue of the same request count. The input loop awaits sends of initialization, parse, duplicate-id, and overload responses. A slow writer/full response queue stops this loop from reading cancellation notifications. List bridge methods have no cancellation argument; other operations rely entirely on bridge cooperation without a cancellation select.\n- Consequence: overload, repeated invalid input, or output backpressure can remove the responsiveness promised by the server comment, and hung list calls cannot be cancelled through the bridge contract. Increasing counts or adding a timeout does not repair the control-plane dependency. Positive-only validation also fails to check the semaphore's representable maximum before constructing it.\n- Repair direction: give control notifications independent progress under response backpressure, define cancellation for every admitted method, and make admission/rejection recoverable with validated resource bounds. No installed MCP-server incident or test execution is claimed.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQJCVSMcFzr5Z4Zk58xpIn2OuAgyKjPiCtuoIEG6OL36Gt0FQVqooR9LmzeUW0OQV4tIuLHMRt+KdPgVG/C0jaww="}
