{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3079,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:50.948371229Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"b4df2c9d-e2cd-582b-b677-bb31b643cd9a","title":"[L529] Candidate creation and restoration scan every directory under a fixed entry limit","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L529.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/runtime/peritus-git/src/snapshot/support.rs:16`, `src/snapshot/support.rs:169-213`, `src/snapshot/operations.rs:28-52`, `src/snapshot/operations.rs:181-224`.\n- Behavior: Before staging a candidate or restoring a snapshot, a synchronous recursive filesystem scan counts every entry, rejecting the 200,001st. It skips descent into the root .git metadata, but does not honor Git ignore rules for other directories. Any nested entry named .git is unsupported, including initialized submodules or nested worktrees. The scan has no resumable cursor, progress or cancellation hook.\n- Consequence: Ignored dependency/build trees can block snapshots and rollback even when their files would not be staged. An initialized submodule is observable through status but its nested .git file blocks candidate creation/restoration. These are fixed compatibility and count barriers, not elapsed deadlines. Restoration subsequently uses read-tree followed by clean -fdx, so its effect contract also includes removing ignored/untracked content from that exact managed worktree.\n- Repair direction: Define candidate content and protected nested repository handling explicitly, and preserve progress for large scans. Avoid turning ignored file volume into a permanent snapshot barrier. Restoration needs a durable planned effect and reconciliation, rather than weakening repository ownership protection. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-423