{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3103,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.005772019Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"a5b536da-05f8-52e1-90b7-711749c02cad","title":"[L542] Proxy recovery records classify supplied facts rather than reconnecting owned sockets","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L542.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/runtime/peritus-network/src/recovery.rs:20-94`, `src/proxy/mod.rs:71-79`, `src/proxy/mod.rs:153-188`, `src/proxy/inherited.rs:32-40`.\n- Behavior: ProxyRecoveryRecord is an in-memory checksummed value. This crate has no record encode/decode or attach/reopen operation that restores a ManagedProxy from it. classify accepts an observed owner and caller-supplied listener/worker facts; it does not inspect the recorded listener port, current plan or routing token. Absent listener/zero workers is AbsentClean only if released was already recorded; otherwise it is Mismatched. Live handles own process-local threads, cancellation tokens, sockets and non-clone routing material and cancel/join on Drop.\n- Consequence: A checksum and LiveOwned classification do not establish exact live reattachment. A crash before recording released does not become a proven clean completion through this classifier. Higher native recovery adapters may provide observation/reconciliation and must be read before claiming a live failure. There is no durable same-context recovery implementation here.\n- Repair direction: Bind recovery to real observed runtime identities and durable intent/completion, with explicit absent/unknown/replacement handling. Keep socket ownership distinct from provider context identity. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOT