{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3131,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.072808346Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"f9a7c46e-3b1a-5a19-ad32-99036ad13b32","title":"[L559] Logical environment and path normalization can disagree with native lookup semantics","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L559.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/runtime/peritus-sandbox/src/environment.rs:13-29`, `src/environment.rs:94-102`, `src/filesystem.rs:17-43`, `src/filesystem.rs:52-59`, `src/backend.rs:79-98`, `tests/contracts.rs:17-26`.\n- Behavior: Every environment name is uppercased, including names destined for case-sensitive Unix environments. Logical paths uppercase only a drive letter, trim trailing separators, and compare other path bytes exactly; they retain repeated interior separators and do not incorporate native Windows case equivalence. Contracts use those logical values for exact or descendant matching.\n- Consequence: A valid Unix mixed-case environment variable can become a different lookup name, and equivalent native path spellings can receive different logical decisions. Native backend translation may impose additional rules; this is a contract/translation risk, not a confirmed installed failure.\n- Repair direction: Bind canonicalization to the admitted native semantics and preserve the original native identity, while checking traversal and authority on the canonical native object.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQGp75GCP43RXUfimWyH3VNG98XW9WyI36i1KT96c3ZRmegmC