{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3133,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.077108606Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"5305f62c-c89f-52d9-b7c2-785aaaed5c7a","title":"[L560] Public IP-prefix construction bypasses the checked constructor and can panic during matching","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L560.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/runtime/peritus-sandbox/src/network.rs:106-165`, `src/network.rs:167-178`, `src/network.rs:226-235`, `src/network.rs:276-314`, `src/canonical.rs:138-152`, `tests/contracts.rs:79-103`.\n- Behavior: The public `HostMatcher::IpPrefix` variant exposes address and prefix length directly. Its helper constructor checks IPv4 lengths at most 32 and IPv6 lengths at most 128, but rule and contract constructors do not revalidate directly supplied variants. Matching computes shifts using the supplied length, and canonical encoding accepts its byte unchanged.\n- Consequence: A directly constructed invalid prefix can reach arithmetic/shift failure during evaluation instead of a typed invalid-input result. Non-normalized prefixes can also carry noncanonical identities. The existing test uses the checked helper and does not exercise this bypass.\n- Repair direction: Make prefix values opaque and checked, or validate every public ingress before canonicalization and matching, preserving valid protocol prefix bounds.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQHkEA9SnbxI7Yg1OAK3M4cIWoBiyXl0b8bj74DbHDaMyDPnIWNntxxCCz5EOS7BTdwzzAGCeBtUfy5FO5qM2FQg="}
