 and nonregression: long-running commands, output and stdin pressure, stalled helper/input readers, control-queue saturation, late start/exit races, parent loss and restart, proving cancellation responsiveness, no PID-reuse adoption and no duplicate process dispatch. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQDGSJCFVrMTMsV3fiYTtYxV5swf0aqtcjDN1X600dmiz9E4q3EbouyaSWo3b8noB/denMceqUDB3TOM9UOWtTwM="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3139,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.090455654Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"31c11fbe-5dbc-58e0-b406-ce5eb7b6b1cb","title":"[L564] Linux cgroups impose a fixed CPU throttle alongside configured resource limits","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L564.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/runtime/peritus-sandbox-linux/src/cgroup.rs:90-143`; `src/native/rlimit.rs:1-35`; `src/descriptor.rs:105-154` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: Every prepared cgroup receives `cpu.max = 100000 100000`, regardless of the requested CPU-time budget. Preparation requires delegation of CPU, memory, and process controllers together. Native rlimits separately impose configured address-space, file-size, file-descriptor, process, and CPU bounds; a zero CPU duration skips that rlimit.\n- Consequence: An untimed execution can still be throttled to one CPU. Missing any required controller rejects preparation. The disk allocation is applied as a per-file `RLIMIT_FSIZE`, which does not enforce the aggregate disk meaning of that allocation. These are source properties, not measurements of the installed daemon.\n- Repair direction: Represent CPU bandwidth separately from CPU duration and allow an explicitly unbounded bandwidth policy. Require only the controls necessary for the authorized plan, preserving containment. Give aggregate disk accounting its own enforcement and recovery semantics.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQAFkFiO9pX3DfTK3VbwvcIFGy3SHr5lJWMhX7cm8VO5YDgvvRO/qH4tOH/xJgeNdhSIVkQXMVqflei0ZUqQSDQM="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3140,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.092643230Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"31c11fbe-5dbc-58e0-b406-ce5eb7b6b1cb","comment_uuid":"fe25efe7-c350-4bd4-a2d2-4060e4d3b1ac","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L564: Linux cgroups impose a fixed CPU throttle alongside configured resource limits.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: crates/runtime/peritus-sandbox-linux/src/cgroup.rs:90-143; src/native/rlimit.rs:1-35; src/descriptor.rs:105-154. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\nRepresent CPU bandwidth separately from CPU duration and allow an explicitly unbounded bandwidth policy. Require only the controls necessary for the authorized plan, preserving containment. Give aggregate disk accounting its own enforcement and recovery semantics.\n\nPreserve: exact process birth/tree identity, confined authority, one-use dispatch receipts, monotonic cancel intent, truthful terminal/ownership settlement, complete output accounting and platform OS constraints. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: long-running commands, output and stdin pressure, stalled helper/input readers, control-queue saturation, late start/exit races, parent loss and restart, proving cancellation responsiveness, no PID-reuse adoption and no duplicate process dispatch. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQC99NXE7hPUF0Aat+kR8YlHZgLg6ODiyLPXCoF/zkAq5OUkkyCOZ4hecx3dHKnJo0SVeb2/LhMlBNb582OV22w0="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3141,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.095581865Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"5cb72961-67d4-57c8-9f83-8fe672ac81e7","title":"[L565] Linux cgroup cleanup has a three-second cutoff and can lose its retry owner","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L565.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/runtime/peritus-sandbox-linux/src/cgroup.rs:11-12,129-143,176-242`; `src/preparation.rs:284-328` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: Cleanup issues `cgroup.kill` when available, polls population, and returns a retry error after three seconds. Drop ignores that error. Failed installation ignores removal errors before a handle is returned. Existence checks can interpret inaccessible metadata as absence, and population parsing treats a missing or malformed populated field as unpopulated. Higher-level process error mapping collapses detailed cleanup causes into supervisor failures.\n- Consequence: A synthetic cutoff can leave a populated leaf after the in-memory owner disappears. Partial preparation can leave a leaf that the next installation rejects as already existing. Ambiguous observations can also be mistaken for completed cleanup.\n- Repair direction: Persist the exact cleanup obligation before side effects and retain it until verified reconciliation succeeds. Treat inaccessible or malformed kernel state as indeterminate. Use progress notifications and explicit cancellation instead of abandoning ownership after a timer.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZ