{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3143,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.100372580Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"58a82a3f-4e3d-5be0-91fc-bbf40fa167cf","title":"[L566] Linux recovery classifies a cgroup but cannot restore a native session","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L566.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/runtime/peritus-sandbox-linux/src/recovery.rs:32-55,128-174,194-279`; `src/session.rs:32-44` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: The recovery record stores identifiers and an optional root PID/birth token. Classification needs that root to remain identifiable, searches ancestry for at most 64 links, accepts live ownership when one member descends from the root, and rejects an empty existing leaf as mismatched. Cleanup is admitted only for the live-owned classification. The crate supplies no native-session reconstruction, stream reattachment, or resume operation.\n- Consequence: A dead original root, an empty leaf, or sufficiently deep ancestry prevents this cleanup path from reconciling the recorded execution. A record is not persistent execution or a restored context window. Accepting one matching member also does not establish ownership of every member.\n- Repair direction: Define durable execution ownership independently of the launching root. Reconcile every exact member and empty/partially prepared state, and restore streams, lifecycle state, and protocol cursors through an explicit attachment contract.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQM+nmDRv