{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3145,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.104887664Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"8d92aba1-52f9-5eae-ab95-5e7a382ae409","title":"[L567] Linux child execution remains bound to the launching process","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L567.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/runtime/peritus-sandbox-linux/src/runner.rs:53-88`; `src/session.rs:32-44,219-257`; `src/filesystem.rs:316-347` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: Bubblewrap is launched with `--die-with-parent`, a new session, and fresh namespaces. The native session holds launch handles, execution-status ownership, cgroup ownership, proxy ownership, and secret delivery in process memory.\n- Consequence: Losing the launcher terminates its child and loses the objects needed to attach to that execution. A recovery record cannot preserve those namespaces, handles, or output streams by itself.\n- Repair direction: Move custody of execution and its handles to a durable supervisor with an authenticated attachment protocol. Separate client disconnection from execution cancellation and preserve provider continuation identity alongside execution identity.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQJtCG+mdEdgl8qKcPpP4OndAFtne8T0dYzJeWgAGV0hIO6XD5D+9r4UUnI5exZfi8UIAU8aRkvcDRpucv1RIYwc="}
