{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3153,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.123506119Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"ef65e4fd-88b1-5d43-a0e5-92c966d642bf","title":"[L571] Linux native manifests have fixed bounds that can conflict with accepted plans","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L571.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/runtime/peritus-sandbox-linux/src/canonical.rs:5-12`; `src/manifest.rs:62-68,109-144,220-230`; `src/manifest/value.rs:20-30`; `src/filesystem.rs:316-347`; `src/runner.rs:80-88` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: Canonical payloads are capped at 1 MiB, collections at 256, and protected payload bindings at 128. Command arguments are capped at 256 with 64 KiB per argument. Manifest rule admission caps the native rule list at 256; ambient-root projection can add a rule to an otherwise accepted 256-rule plan. Some configured paths are serialized with lossy UTF-8 conversion.\n- Consequence: A logically accepted plan can fail during native manifest construction after preparation has begun. Non-UTF-8 configured paths can be changed by serialization rather than rejected at the boundary. These are schema and integrity bounds, not session lifetimes.\n- Repair direction: Check complete native representability before allocating resources, version larger or streamed representations when needed, and use lossless platform path identity. Preserve finite framing limits where the transport needs them and report exact unsupported dimensions.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3Nza