{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3155,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.127990750Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"23c0bd24-69d0-5466-91b8-bdcb584eb8db","title":"[L572] Linux syscall admission is a fixed compatibility gate","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L572.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/runtime/peritus-sandbox-linux/src/native/seccomp_policy.rs:7-146,165-171`; `src/native/helper.rs:86-93` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: The seccomp policy uses a static syscall allowlist with architecture additions; all other calls receive errno 1. There is no workload-specific extension path. The policy omits several classic x86 filesystem and event APIs. Its unit qualification verifies compilation for two architectures rather than representative daily workloads.\n- Consequence: An otherwise authorized program using an omitted syscall can fail inside the sandbox. This is a compatibility blocker, not a timer; actual failures depend on the executable and libc paths used.\n- Repair direction: Ground syscall requirements in supported workloads and selected capabilities, qualify actual toolchains, and retain explicit denial of operations outside the authorization envelope.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQNNLC30GZxu087kKoCGaR77QD1MqAz8XfyAdvdPPzWsD07j1m/QlmP5qevx4k4uQlxdZ+0Wr0hafvsN0qGZzjw8="}
