{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3157,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.132464453Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"837fecee-a636-5c5e-bc63-86c17b0fe37b","title":"[L573] Linux release can lose proxy ownership and hides actionable recovery causes","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L573.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/runtime/peritus-sandbox-linux/src/session.rs:15,171-197,272-311`; `src/network.rs:69-83`; `src/preparation.rs:160-167,284-328` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: Release preserves its cgroup handle on cleanup failure but proxy shutdown takes the proxy owner before the fallible shutdown completes. Several native preparation errors are mapped into generic supervisor or replan failures. The rich observation history has a fixed 64-event cap, checked after appending a common observation.\n- Consequence: Proxy failure can leave release without the object needed for a same-owner retry, and callers lose the detailed retry/repair classification. The observation cap is latent: the currently closed legal lifecycle cannot exhaust 64 events, so it is not evidence of a daily session expiration.\n- Repair direction: Retain or durably transfer ownership until cleanup completion is acknowledged, preserve typed recovery causes, and make observation append atomic if future lifecycle expansion can reach its bound.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQO0XUbicU3zuQH4m4Go+XK2vsdGQjUtnCNRTVATj9FgEadHEHyLSafKDaOqYYiWsBgUvrSezQilESXoR+WAXBQ0="}
