{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3345,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.571062402Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"d396555a-433c-5d64-9eb2-7ef70ef7f5ee","title":"[L700] Wire terminal values are copied before attachment-specific size checks and accept dimensions wider than native PTY support","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L700.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/app/peritus-app-protocol/src/wire/terminal.rs:47-76,87-97,109-117,170-183`; `src/wire/error.rs:11-44` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: Terminal input/output first read owned bytes under generic codec bounds, then reject lengths above the negotiated terminal chunk ceiling. Resize decoding uses u16::MAX for both dimensions, while native Windows conversion in L611 has narrower i16 support. Wire errors preserve code/retry/subsystem and bounded diagnostic, but do not carry the local typed codec source/offset.\n- Consequence: Domain-invalid chunks allocate before rejection, and protocol-valid resize values can fail later in the native adapter. Wire transport preserves more structured recovery information than Display in L696, although low-level local codec detail is lost across that representation.\n- Repair direction: Check domain length before allocating, negotiate dimensions that the selected backend can support, and retain useful bounded structured causal diagnostics. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQCubl3l/D9wFfiXxWSAJfsoDl1Du1NI0QAfbwufEcYg3JgT1vsQ1JMhA5PuIC02pv7tWZnYhJl/OiX0Mk0ca