{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3353,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.588709207Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"51ffd7fa-1be8-5f52-a09f-3ba120d6c202","title":"[L704] Context inspection has fixed source-size and sealed-message ordinal gates","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L704.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/app/peritus-app-protocol/src/workbench/context.rs:11-12,41-50,174-196,202-217,246-264,300-342`; `src/wire/workbench_context.rs:87-137`; `src/workbench/context/tests.rs:28-89` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: Context metadata pages contain exactly up to 32 rows at a revision fence, with u32 total/offset and no 8,192-row offset ceiling (tests admit u32::MAX queries). Metadata rows reject source sizes over 64 MiB and sealed message ordinals at or above 4,096, even though the response carries only digests/byte counts. Empty files are allowed but other zero-byte sources are rejected. Seals bind invocation/request/manifest/generation values; generation itself is not validated in the seal constructor and there is no provider-native context identifier in this record. Wire checks page count before allocating.\n- Consequence: A sufficiently large retained source/request can become uninspectable through this DTO independently of actual available storage or provider capacity. A sealed request manifest is not proof that a provider context window was restored. Pagination/revision checks protect honest coverage and should be retained.\n- Repair direction: Let metadata describe larger durable sources without requiring them to fit model input; use wide indexed positions and bind native restoration separately. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihm