{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3355,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.593148450Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"006c4fee-9275-57c3-b485-c14cd8a5f41c","title":"[L705] User queue text and whole pending-order representations impose independent capacity gates","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L705.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/app/peritus-app-protocol/src/workbench/inputs.rs:5-8,22-29,80-86,103-115,240-249,285-301`; `src/wire/workbench_inputs.rs:26-51,148-160`; `src/workbench/intent.rs:18-31,92-115` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: Each user input revision is capped at 8,192 UTF-8 bytes; queue pages are 32 rows with exact revision/total coverage. Order/dependency lists use at most 65,535 exact IDs; wire reads raw u16 count into Vec capacity without the generic negotiated collection check. Reorder names the complete pending set. Queue acceptance/correction and explicit execution are separate typed operations; archive/pin metadata do not themselves cancel execution or erase history.\n- Consequence: Long daily-driving instructions fail as malformed input despite larger task-string limits elsewhere. Pending-set growth can block a complete reorder and produce frame/allocation pressure; paging queue history alone does not solve whole-order mutation. These are byte/cardinality gates, not elapsed sessions.\n- Repair direction: Retain exact immutable inputs while supporting larger text through durable artifacts/chunks and indexed order mutations; preserve explicit holds, dependency checks and execution authority. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1st