{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3375,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.635537933Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"5e30b9c0-76c3-530b-aa48-7d29afbbc53d","title":"[L716] Continuation lacks the durable operation identity carried by ordinary workbench commands","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L716.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/app/peritus-app-protocol/src/wire/workbench.rs:25-43,67-84,138-147,171-185`; `src/wire/workbench/intent.rs:1-114`; `src/wire/workbench/intent/decode.rs:1-184`; `src/wire/workbench/intent/encode.rs:1-185` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: Ordinary workbench commands and their receipts include a control-operation ID, scope, revision and digest independent of transport request/correlation IDs. The separate continuation request contains only WorkbenchQuery and mode; it carries no durable operation ID, expected revision, run/settings digest or provider invocation/context ownership identity. Its encoding cannot express an exact accepted-operation retry receipt.\n- Consequence: A continuation retry cannot be fenced and resolved using the same command identity at this boundary. Whether the host synthesizes and durably records an equivalent identity remains to be traced; this DTO is not proof of repeated execution or actual installed session loss.\n- Repair direction: Route continuation through the same durable command/receipt lifecycle, with explicit native context ownership and reconciliation after transport loss. Retain scope and revision authority checks. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAA