{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3393,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.675289771Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"9c172db5-d0a9-5465-9a4f-fafdce4cbec0","title":"[L729] Terminal owners are removed before join succeeds and unrelated reap failures gate new work","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L729.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/terminal/registry.rs:94-117,258-288,323-343`; `src/terminal/registry/retirement.rs:1-34`; `src/terminal/bridge.rs:301-338`; `src/terminal/registry/launch.rs:15-82` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: registration first reaps other completed processes and propagates an error from that scan. Retirement removes owner/index entries before waiting for the native owner. Shutdown clears all attachment/receipt maps and takes all bridges before sequential cancellation/waits, returning only the first error.\n- Consequence: another process's completion failure can block admission; failed joins or cleanup no longer have a registry owner to retry. An unlimited wait can also hold shutdown indefinitely. Separate native-process journal facts may still exist; the loss identified here is the terminal registry's recovery/control state.\n- Repair direction: journal reaping/shutdown intent and keep failed owners discoverable until exact terminal and cleanup receipts reconcile. Isolate per-process recovery failures from unrelated admission and retain cancellable observation without inventing execution deadlines. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1M