{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3399,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.688842956Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"5f4d5c19-13cd-5d18-8aba-3456bb2bc85c","title":"[L732] Telemetry export has fixed replay and spool-entry gates on its recovery path","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L732.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/telemetry/runtime.rs:21-63`; `src/telemetry/local_file.rs:13,21-75,79-110,118-138,156-169` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: open recovers all trace records, projects them, restores eight retained checkpoints, and rebuilds a 4,096-item DropOldest buffer with 128-item batches before flushing synchronously. The exporter rejects a directory above 65,536 entries; its count includes non-batches and quarantined temporaries. Quarantine renames incomplete files but does not remove them; byte quota eviction selects only batch-prefixed files. Windows directory sync is inert.\n- Consequence: retained non-batch/incomplete entries can exhaust admission regardless of remaining byte quota. Export/recovery I/O can block open and propagates Worker/Retry errors rather than providing an independent unavailable-observation mode. Fixed dropping and checkpoint retention need explicit gap accounting; they are not execution timers. Existing-file reads verify size first but then read to EOF without a read cap.\n- Repair direction: recover and compact spool entries with explicit durable export/gap receipts; make telemetry failure observable without silently losing primary work progress. Page trace recovery and keep filesystem I/O off progress-critical paths. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signatur