{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3431,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.759775569Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"b5069336-2e5b-55e6-9f23-8e05b0f0381a","title":"[L749] Refresh-on-request reobserves every selected source and can repeatedly invalidate preparation","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L749.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/product_run/workbench/files/refresh.rs:15-112`; `src/product_run/workbench/files.rs:75-146`; `src/product_run/workbench/files/page.rs:11-65` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: each request refresh loads the full execution record, captures inputs, clones all eligible refresh references and calls prepare_file for each. The comparison includes the whole source digest/size and selected range digest. Each changed source is committed separately and advances the conversation revision, requiring request reconstruction. Errors from source preparation collapse to generic Unavailable. Inspection returns 32 rows after loading the full archive.\n- Consequence: changing sources can keep preparation in repeated read/rebuild cycles; multiple changes can commit a partial refresh set before a later error. Changes outside the selected range still change the source observation. There is no artificial elapsed deadline in this loop, but absence of a stable preparation snapshot can prevent forward progress.\n- Repair direction: define a durable refresh generation and exact selected-source snapshot, publish its changes atomically where possible, and resume preparation at a verified boundary. Retain explicit refresh intent and folder/path protection; surface the particular failed source. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPa