{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3445,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.790545130Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"50ef498e-e34c-572c-94ed-64ad21e7cdc0","title":"[L756] Improvement schema handling quarantines old state without a resumable migration transaction","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L756.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/app/peritus-daemon/src/product_run/improvements/store.rs:121-137,283-336`; `src/product_run/improvements/store/tests.rs:114-163` in the same crate.\n- Behavior: schema 1 is renamed into improvements-quarantine and replaced with an empty schema-2 inbox; other nonzero unsupported versions fail open. Quarantine moves SQLite sidecars one by one before the main database, then syncs directories and writes a synchronous diagnostic. This function stores no move intent or recovery cursor. The fixture covers successful quarantine and retained legacy bytes; Windows directory sync is inert.\n- Consequence: a failure between renames can leave a partially moved database/sidecar set; successful quarantine preserves files but does not expose their suggestions/evaluation reservations in the current inbox. A future schema remains an explicit compatibility blocker. Clean reopen coverage does not prove interrupted migration repair.\n- Repair direction: retain a durable migration/quarantine manifest and reconcile every database/sidecar move before opening the successor store; offer evidence-grounded legacy recovery. Never reinterpret unknown schema bytes as current authority or discard old state. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN