{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3493,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.903256738Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"ab829f7f-d97c-54cd-810a-0500850e2a33","title":"[L808] Web consoles are process-local and counted even after the CLI exits","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L808.\n\n- Evidence: `crates/app/peritus-web/src/terminal.rs:29-131`; `src/state.rs:55-62,114-120`, `src/consoles.rs:19-88`, `src/api.rs:307-310,357-389` in that crate.\n- Behavior: the gateway stores only in-memory PTY owners and refuses the 25th console even if existing children have ended. Output beyond 1 MiB is discarded with an explicit lost-offset flag, delivered in 64 KiB pages. CLI arguments cap at 128 and 32,768 bytes each; terminal input caps at 65,536 bytes and geometry at 10–400 columns/2–200 rows. Drop kills and waits for the direct child synchronously, with no owned persistent adopt record or joined reader thread.\n- Consequence: gateway exit discards console identities and output and terminates CLI children on ordinary Drop; a restarted gateway starts with an empty map. Finished consoles consume capacity until explicitly closed. A blocked input write or child wait can hold an async handler or the consoles map lock indefinitely. Retaining a map entry during one gateway lifetime is not persistent session ownership.\n- Repair direction: separate display subscriptions from durable process ownership, persist console binding/output offsets with explicit close disposition, and make child/group lifecycle and input waits observable and cancelable without expiration.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoL