{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3507,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.935484416Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"0aea9a98-4f97-5ce2-9d8d-d0e2087a64e5","title":"[L816] Browser console interruption shares an unresolved input queue","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L816.\n\n- Evidence: `webui/src/lib/components/Console.svelte:1-41`; process-local gateway console and output limits in L808.\n- Behavior: Console input, including Ctrl-C bytes, is chained behind previous input promises. The input field clears before acknowledgement without a durable original. Terminal scrollback is 4000 lines. A new component starts reading at offset zero, and each next read waits for the previous request before applying its 250-ms or 1800-ms cadence.\n- Consequence: An unresolved input request can prevent the later interrupt from reaching the process. Browser history and the gateway's 1-MiB output ring are separate eviction boundaries; the 64-KiB page and polling cadence can lose output when production outruns retrieval. Closing a panel does not transfer native process ownership or preserve its output. Cadences are not execution deadlines.\n- Repair direction: Give interruption an independent owner/control path, retain input effect receipts, and use durable output with acknowledged offsets rather than relying on process-local rings and browser scrollback. No tests run.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQAi4z9hdfyuYxAb+bYvsRolRVymrBUy7oo/TCVkOcl2HY8g5KCCiJoO6XlqOzcj