{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3521,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.966659372Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"7c3f9584-61b7-5a09-b45b-23367a28355e","title":"[L857] Installed service templates impose restart and stop limits","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L857.\n\n- Source: `packaging/linux/peritus.service:4-13`; `packaging/windows/Peritus.Task.xml.in:20-47`; `packaging/macos/com.corvidae.peritus.plist.in:14-22`.\n- Linux permits **five starts within 300 seconds**, restarts failures after **five seconds**, and sets a **40-second stop timeout** with mixed kill mode. Windows has no total execution-time limit (`PT0S`), but allows only **five failure restarts** at five-second intervals and permits hard termination. macOS throttles relaunch to **five seconds** and restarts unsuccessful exits.\n- Daily-driving consequence: Linux and Windows can exhaust automatic recovery after repeated daemon failures, leaving reconnection dependent on external intervention. Stop deadlines can also interrupt graceful recovery. These are checked-in templates; installation of these registrations on the original live instance has not been established.\n- Repair direction: preserve daemon and task identity through recoverable failures, remove fixed restart exhaustion, and coordinate shutdown with durable recovery receipts. No service or process was changed.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQI0bZgi4yUw3IWGQmAslaJjRHfgRDLbhulRkqelIDFeqNx6CBR7+eFK1noB5Nhmq5gRKPngBEg