{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3523,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.970896341Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"a31cc400-cf17-5509-94a3-c4943c4e7809","title":"[L858] Upgrades use disposable rollback state and lack a durable session handoff","description":"Source finding: docs/daily-driving-limiters-audit.md — L858.\n\n- Evidence: full `packaging/linux/Upgrade-Peritus.sh`, `packaging/macos/Upgrade-Peritus.sh`, `packaging/windows/Upgrade-Peritus.ps1`; `packaging/linux/Install-Peritus.sh:83-98`; `packaging/macos/Install-Peritus.sh:36-45`; `packaging/windows/Install-Peritus.ps1:90-108`; `packaging/linux/Uninstall-Peritus.sh:17-44`.\n- Behavior: all upgrades copy package files into a temporary backup, invoke the installer, then delete the backup on success or rollback handling. Linux suppresses restore-copy failures and still deletes the backup; macOS removes the application tree before restoring; Windows's finally block deletes backup even when restoration throws. These scripts have no persistent transaction journal, startup recovery, or provider/native session handoff. POSIX installers atomically publish individual files but neither coordinate a multi-file version transaction nor stop the running daemon; Windows installation force-stops matching installed processes first. Linux uninstall coordinates only its registered systemd service, then removes package files.\n- Consequence: interruption outside trap/exception handling can leave a mixed installed generation, and failed rollback can erase the retained recovery source. Successful POSIX replacement can coexist with an older running executable; Windows replacement can interrupt active sessions without a checkpoint handshake. User data preservation messages do not demonstrate preservation of native ownership or the provider context window.\n- Repair direction: persist installation generation and rollback receipts until recovery is verified, and coordinate upgrades through an exact-instance durable checkpoint/reconnect protocol. Preserve backup on failed restoration and resume interrupted publication instead of relying on disposable scope cleanup. Source inspection only; no upgrade, uninstall, or restart executed.\n\nScope: implement and verify the systemic repair recorded in the decision comment. Source-derived behavior remains distinct from a reproduced live incident.","priority":"high","labels":["daily-driving-limiter"],"parent_uuid":"f89861db-18b8-4237-83e6-344f4e472dd4","created_by":"6ME5"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQBRavDF8iKfzs5ODeqwEKj4Ku2VN/2wlASP4dx0onOdTNQO4UaxjykbUyPikPsEqzqtvGwMmGS1um10mcaYj+wY="}
{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3524,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:27:51.973132270Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"a31cc400-cf17-5509-94a3-c4943c4e7809","comment_uuid":"3cc6ade6-5303-4b46-a7a9-aa7da96efe99","author":"6ME5","content":"Systemic repair plan for L858: Upgrades use disposable rollback state and lack a durable session handoff.\n\nOwning contract and implementation boundary: packaging/linux/Upgrade-Peritus.sh; packaging/macos/Upgrade-Peritus.sh; packaging/linux/Install-Peritus.sh:83-98; packaging/macos/Install-Peritus.sh:36-45; packaging/linux/Uninstall-Peritus.sh:17-44. Apply the change through the constructors, live callers, durable writer and restart reader for this boundary, not as an isolated constant increase.\n\npersist installation generation and rollback receipts until recovery is verified, and coordinate upgrades through an exact-instance durable checkpoint/reconnect protocol. Preserve backup on failed restoration and resume interrupted publication instead of relying on disposable scope cleanup. Source inspection only; no upgrade, uninstall, or restart executed.\n\nPreserve: committed command/event identity, exact causal and digest roots, exclusive ownership, atomic publication, reference-safe artifact retention and verified recovery from the last durable frontier. Separate a physical allocation/page limit and a genuine authority or integrity check from a cumulative work allowance. Keep transient contention/capacity observable and recoverable under the existing logical task; do not discard accepted state or substitute a fresh session to get around it.\n\nCompatibility/migration: retain decoders and exact receipts for previously accepted records. If representation or lifecycle semantics change, introduce a versioned migration under exclusive ownership; verify a complete replacement frontier before atomic publication and keep the old recovery root until adoption is durable. Rebuild derived projections from authoritative evidence. Update all producers, consumers and invariants together; a rejected new operation must leave the predecessor usable and already owned effects reconcilable.\n\nAcceptance and nonregression: history beyond the reported count/byte boundaries, lock contention lasting beyond the old patience window, lost commit acknowledgements followed by later progress, torn uncommitted tails and true committed corruption, then reopen and resolve exact historical receipts. Exercise the precise source-described failure path and the relevant byte/count/elapsed boundaries listed in this finding, plus ordinary small inputs. Check that legitimate work progresses after the former blocker while malformed input, conflicting identity, stale preimages and unauthorized scope still reject. Add fault injection at the affected acceptance/publication/recovery boundary and run the affected integration and formal checks on the final implementation. Source-derived risks remain distinct from executed reproductions; completion requires this evidence, not a promise that a plan alone cannot break anything.","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:49.900Z","kind":"decision"},"signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B/HR//do+oIeI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQq5MtA3gDv1stiLN1YhgJ9dMhoOBjZhbEednuPkV+1IAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQFrFLx65jHobkoXLgHdXoIpDkZythcNKXRjo/hvAeAVcYlCYP2zBJdrZXyBLufg/NkHH2K7lVU4ElStV/vOzqQw="}
