{"agent_id":"6ME5","agent_seq":3615,"timestamp":"2026-10-06T22:03:31.429525551Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"6b00f6d1-ae7b-41bd-990d-d7c4f66679f9","comment_uuid":"fda5d9c5-fcbf-49fb-b1d7-e250076b85e1","author":"6ME5","content":"Source-grounded diagnosis: the old stdout reader awaits write_all plus sync_data after every <=8 KiB pipe read. This couples child pipe progress to every storage durability barrier even though stdin/stdout/stderr/wait are polled concurrently. The failing Windows log only proves the ten-second fixture watchdog expired; it does not distinguish a permanent deadlock from slow persistence. The identical transport now passed native Windows jobs 112508942336 and 112512922614 (first fixture about 1.70 s), so this is intermittent, not an always-reproducible pipe deadlock. Repair the shared progress invariant: an owned journal writer receives ordered chunks, coalesces immediately available chunks, and syncs independently of pipe drainage. Keep a finite physical backlog with backpressure, never a total-output or elapsed quota. Reserve backlog ownership before reading so cancellation cannot discard a received chunk waiting to enqueue. Cancellation/error drops pipe ownership, kills and reaps the child, and settles already accepted journal work before returning. No detached tasks, schema change, global-session fallback, increased fixture watchdog, or restored production deadline. Compare alternative EOF-only batching: rejected because quiet thread-start prefixes must remain recoverable while inference is still running. Verify deterministic delayed persistence, exact ordered bytes, persistence failures, quiet prefixes, cancellation/reaping, large concurrent stdin/stdout/stderr, adapter suites, strict lint, Windows compilation and native runner evidence. Actual storage latency in the original failing runner remains unmeasured.","created_at":"2026-10-06T22:03:31.425244281Z","kind":"decision","signed_by":"SHA256:eV8eZPaZxut5mrkihmvsOTrGWClwD+B